1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,919
Boot gave you an executable web app. Now a
product manager asks for an HTTP API:
create orders, fetch orders, return
sensible errors.

2
00:00:10,039 --> 00:00:25,479
The practical situation is mapping HTTP
into method calls without turning
controllers into dumping grounds. Spring
MVC maps HTTP to methods — be intentional
about DTOs, validation, and status codes.

3
00:00:25,599 --> 00:00:35,920
Look at this java for a moment.
@RestController combines controller
semantics with response-body conversion.
@GetMapping binds a path and verb.

4
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:46,679
Behind the scenes, DispatcherServlet is
the front controller: it receives the
request, finds a handler, invokes it, and
works with message converters and
exception resolvers.

5
00:00:46,799 --> 00:01:00,640
Request arrives; handler mapping selects a
method; argument resolvers bind path
variables, query params, and bodies; your
method runs; return value handlers write
the response; exception resolvers
translate failures.

6
00:01:00,759 --> 00:01:13,519
When a response looks wrong, ask which
stage failed — binding, handler logic,
conversion, or exception translation —
instead of randomly tweaking annotations.
Validation belongs at the edge.

7
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:26,280
Annotate request bodies with constraints
and trigger validation deliberately —
@Valid / @Validated at the right parameter
— so bad input fails with 400-class
responses instead of corrupting the
domain.

8
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:37,360
Pick an error structure: code, message,
fields, correlation id — and reuse it.
Centralize with controller advice so every
controller does not invent its own
try/catch theater.

9
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:49,480
When validation fails, do not leak stack
traces. When authorization fails, do not
reveal whether a resource exists if that
revelation is itself sensitive.
Controllers sit at a trust boundary.

10
00:01:49,600 --> 00:02:02,520
ResponseEntity lets you control status
codes and headers when a bare return type
is too blunt. Be boring and correct: 201
for creation with Location when
applicable, 404 when missing, 409 for
conflicts.

11
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:15,440
Controllers that always return 200 with an
error flag reinvent HTTP poorly. Bare
strings are fine for hello world;
production APIs need deliberate envelopes.
Do not expose entities blindly.

12
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:29,719
Returning JPA entities with lazy fields is
a classic footgun: serialization triggers
lazy loads, or LazyInitializationException
after the session closes. Schema leakage
causes API churn when columns change.

13
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:44,120
Request DTOs validate input; response DTOs
shape output; domain entities stay inward.
Controllers that accept entities invite
over-posting — clients setting fields they
should never touch. Walk a create-order
endpoint.

14
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:56,480
Validate the request DTO. Call the
service. Return 201 with a Location header
and a response DTO. On domain conflict,
return 409 with the shared error shape.

15
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:09,080
Walk one failure from production: a list
endpoint returns entities; under load the
session closes, Jackson serializes, a lazy
collection triggers, and the API throws
five hundred.

16
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:17,680
The fix is not "open session in view
forever" without debate — it is a DTO
query or an explicit fetch tailored to the
response.

17
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:29,880
MVC and JPA meet at this seam; Episode
Seventy-Five deepens persistence, but the
controller already should have refused to
spill entities. REST is not only JSON over
HTTP.

18
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:45,039
Resource modeling matters: nouns for
resources, verbs as HTTP methods, status
codes as outcomes. An endpoint named
/doCreateOrder that always returns 200 is
a remote procedure call wearing a thin
disguise.

19
00:03:45,159 --> 00:03:50,879
Prefer /orders with POST and a 201.
Controller return types in an interview?

20
00:03:51,060 --> 00:04:00,580
Body for simple success, ResponseEntity
when status and headers matter, reactive
types when you are on a reactive stack —
always choose status and errors
deliberately.

21
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:08,619
Mention deliberate status and error
handling, not only "we return the object
and Spring Jacksonifies it." APIs need
durable data.

22
00:04:08,739 --> 00:04:16,699
Spring Data speeds repositories, but you
still own SQL reality, transactions, and
N+1 — and that ownership gap is the next
pressure.
